===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|stuf}}, {{m|enm|stuffe}}, from Anglo-{{der|en|la|stuffa}} and its etymon {{der|en|fro|estofe}}, {{m|fro|estoffe}}, {{m|fro|estuf}}, {{m|fro|estuffe}}, {{m|fro|stoffe}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=stuf(fe|pos=n|id=MED43448}}</ref> from {{m|fro|estoffer}}, {{m|fro|estofer|t=to provide what is necessary, equip, stuff}}, borrowed from {{der|en|goh|stoffōn}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*stoppōn|t=to clog up, block, fill}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=6841369943}}</ref><ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|pos=n|v=22216}}</ref> More at {{m|en|stop}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} [[miscellaneous|Miscellaneous]] items or objects; (with possessive) [[personal effects]].
#: {{ux|en|What is all that '''stuff''' on your bedroom floor?<br>He didn't want his pockets to bulge so he was walking around with all his '''stuff''' in his hands.}}
#* {{RQ:Rinehart Hopwood Bat|I|01|passage=The Bat—they called him the Bat.{{nb...}}. He'd never been in stir, the bulls had never mugged him, he didn't run with a mob, he played a lone hand, and fenced his '''stuff''' so that even the fence couldn't swear he knew his face.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|uncountable}} Furniture; goods; domestic vessels or utensils.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1611|title=Bible, 1 Samuel 25:13, KJV|text=and there went up after David about four hundred men; and two hundred abode by the '''stuff'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1630|author={{w|John Hayward (historian)|John Hayward}}|title=The Life and Raigne of King Edward VI|text=He took away locks, and gave away the king's '''stuff'''.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Unspecified things or matters.
#: {{ux|en|I had to do some '''stuff'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2026 July 8|author=Paul Clifton|title=Brunel does the business|journal=RAIL|issue=1065|page=31|text=Andy Jackson puts it more bluntly: "Just because we are off the naughty step doesn't mean we can stop doing '''stuff'''. It's far from 'no more action'.}}
# {{senseid|en|Q35758}} The tangible [[substance]] that goes into the makeup of a physical object.
#: {{syn|en|matter<id:Q35758>|substance<id:stuff>}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|constituents|ingredients|Thesaurus:substance}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1697|author={{w|Sir John Davies (poet)|John Davies}}|title=A Poem on the Immortality of the Soul|text=The workman on his '''stuff''' his skill doth show, / And yet the '''stuff''' gives not the man his skill.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|title=Indian Cookery "Local" for Young Housekeepers: Second Edition|page=67
|passage=Pound an onion, warm a spoonful of ghee and throw in the onion, brown it slightly, add your curry '''stuff''', brown this till it smells pleasantly, {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} A material for making [[clothing]]; any woven [[textile]], but especially a [[woollen]] fabric.
##* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=51|volume=III|text=Without waiting for a reply, she unbound the veil from her head, and took off the loose black novice's robe, which she had put over a gray '''stuff''' dress similar to that worn by Lucy.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1857|title=The National Magazine|section=volumes 10-11|page=350|text="And you can buy a dress for your wife off this piece of '''stuff'''," said Lisetta, who had always an eye to business.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Hilary Mantel|title=A Place of Greater Safety|publisher=Harper Perennial 2007|page=147|text=She was going out to buy some lengths of good woollen '''stuff''' for Louise's winter dresses.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} [[board|Boards]] used for [[build]]ing.
## Abstract/figurative substance or [[character]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|3|2|line=91–94|text=When that the poor have cried, Caesar hath wept; / Ambition should be made of sterner '''stuff'''}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=c.1610|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=w:The Tempest|section={{nowrap|Act 4, scene 1, 156–157:}}|text=We are such '''stuff''' / As dreams are made on}}
## {{lb|en|paper-making|dated}} [[pulp|Pulp]]; [[paper]] [[stock]] ground ready for use. When partly ground, it is called {{m|en|half stuff}}.<ref>{{R:Knight AMD}}, p.2432</ref>
# {{lb|en|informal}} {{non-gloss|Used as placeholder, usually for [[material]] of unknown type or name.}}
#: {{syn|en|doodad|thingamabob|Thesaurus:thingy}}
#: {{ux|en|Can I have some of that '''stuff''' on my ice-cream sundae?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=w:George Goodchild|title=Death on the Centre Court|chapter=3|text=It had been his intention to go to Wimbledon, but as he himself said: “Why be blooming well frizzled when you can hear all the results over the wireless.{{...}}You stand by, Janet, and wake me up if they do any of that running commentary '''stuff'''.”}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|url=http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21582516-worlds-thirst-oil-could-be-nearing-peak-bad-news-producers-excellent|title=Yesterday’s fuel|text=The dawn of the oil age was fairly recent. Although the '''stuff''' was used to waterproof boats in the Middle East 6,000 years ago, extracting it in earnest began only in 1859 after an oil strike in Pennsylvania. The first barrels of crude fetched $18 (around $450 at today’s prices).|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180425074221/https://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21582516-worlds-thirst-oil-could-be-nearing-peak-bad-news-producers-excellent|archivedate=25 April 2018}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} {{cap|narcotic}} [[drug]]s, especially [[heroin]].
#: {{syn|en|dope|gear|Thesaurus:recreational drug}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1947|author=William Burroughs|title=letter, 11 March|text=For some idiotic reason the bureaucrats are more opposed to tea than to '''stuff'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975|author=Mary Sanches; Ben G. Blount|title=Sociocultural Dimensions of Language Use|page=47|text=For example, one addict would crack shorts (break and enter cars) and usually obtain just enough stolen goods to buy '''stuff''' and get off just before getting sick.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[medicine]] or [[mixture]]; a [[potion]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|act=V|scene=v|page=397|column=1|passage=I{{...}}did compound for her / A certaine '''ſtuffe''', which being tane, would ceaſe / The preſent powre of life{{...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|sometimes|euphemistic}} Refuse or worthless matter; hence, also, foolish or irrational language.
#: {{syn|en|garbage|rubbish|nonsense|stuff and nonsense|Thesaurus:trash|Thesaurus:nonsense}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't give me any of that 'what-about' '''stuff'''!}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|1|passage=Anger would indite / Such woeful '''stuff''' as I or Shadwell write.}}
#* {{RQ:Carroll Snark|fit=4|text=But the Butcher turned nervous, and dressed himself fine, / With yellow kid gloves and a ruff— / Said he felt it exactly like going to dine, / Which the Bellman declared was all "'''stuff'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1912|title=The Machine|author=w:Upton Sinclair|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3304/pg3304-images.html|passage=Oh, '''stuff''', Julia! I've given up chasing after will-o'-the-wisps like that.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A melted mass of [[turpentine]], [[tallow]], etc., with which the masts, sides, and bottom of a ship are smeared for [[lubrication]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1785| title=Pamphlets on British Shipping. 1785-1861 |author=| page=36| passage=The master, at my earnest solicitation, examined his vessel, and though he prefers the coal tar, yet he told me, there were shells sticking on, and that a very thin coat of '''stuff''', if any, remained.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1822| title=A New System of Naval Architecture |author=William Annesley | page=31| passage=On the last transverse planking, after: caulking and paying, he has laid on a coat of '''stuff''', so hard when cold aš to resist a firm touch, and applied plain paper, then took heated band irons (such as women use) , and passed the iron from the centre of the sheet to the extremities, thus heating the '''stuff''' to make it adhere, pressing out the air, and laying it all flat and united with the course.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012| title=American Honor: The Story of Admiral Charles Stewart |author=Thomas Williams | page=561| passage=While the ships were placed in ordinary “a thick coat of '''stuff'''” was applied to the hulls, and their awnings might be spread or sheds erected to provide some protection from sun and weather.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|criminal argot|dated}} {{cap|money}}.

=====Usage notes=====
* The textile sense is increasingly specialized and sounds dated in everyday contexts. In the UK and Commonwealth it designates the cloth from which legal and academic gowns are made, except for the gowns of Queen's/King's/State Counsel, which are (often in contradistinction) made of [[silk]].

